	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop/610> ;
	dc:title	"Decreased thyroid hormone levels in the brain regulated via transport, metabolism and TR activation leading to decreased cognition and motor function" ;
	dcterms:alternative	"Decreased TH levels leading to developmental neurotoxicity" ;
	dc:source	"AOPWiki" ;
	dcterms:created	"2025-10-08T05:42:02" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2025-10-08T08:04:23" ;
	dc:description	"""Olker et al. (2019) identified 20 DIO2-specific inhibitors using a human recombinant DIO2 enzyme (e.g., tetramethrin, elzasonan). Another typical inhibitor of DIO2 (and DIO1 and 3) is iopanoic acid (IOP), which acts as a substrate of all three DIO isoforms (Renko et al., 2015). In fact, many compounds inhibit all three DIO isoforms. Olker et al. (2019) identified 93 compounds that inhibit DIOs 1, 2 and 3.
""", """A prime example of impairments in learning and memory as the adverse outcome for regulatory action is developmental lead exposure and IQ function in children (Bellinger, 2012). Most methods are well established in the published literature and many have been engaged to evaluate the effects of developmental thyroid disruption. The US EPA and OECD Developmental Neurotoxicity (DNT) Guidelines (OCSPP 870.6300 or OECD TG 426) as well as OECD TG 443 (OECD, 2018) both require testing of learning and memory (USEPA, 1998; OECD, 2007) advising to use the following tests passive avoidance, delayed-matching-to-position for the adult rat and for the infant rat, olfactory conditioning, Morris water maze, Biel or Cincinnati maze, radial arm maze, T-maze, and acquisition and retention of schedule-controlled behavior. These DNT Guidelines have been deemed valid to identify developmental neurotoxicity and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes (Makris et al., 2009).&nbsp;

Also, in the frame of the OECD GD 43 (2008) on reproductive toxicity, learning and memory testing may have potential to be applied in the context of developmental neurotoxicity studies. However, many of the learning and memory tasks used in guideline studies may not readily detect subtle impairments in cognitive function associated with modest degrees of developmental thyroid disruption (Gilbert et al., 2012).&nbsp;

&nbsp;
""" ;
	dc:creator	"""Nathalie T.O.M. Dierichsa,b, Aldert H. Piersmaa, Robin P. Peetersb, W. Edward Visserb, Marcel E. Meimab, Ellen V.S. Hessela

aCentre for Health Protection (GZB), National Institute for Public Health and the Environment (RIVM), Bilthoven, the Netherlands
b Academic Centre for Thyroid Diseases, Department of Internal Medicine, Erasmus Medical Centre, Rotterdam, The Netherlands
""" ;
	aopo:has_key_event	aop.events:2093, aop.events:381, aop.events:385, aop.events:1505, aop.events:2105, aop.events:2266, aop.events:2378, aop.events:2377, aop.events:386, aop.events:2108, aop.events:2258, aop.events:2376, aop.events:1002, aop.events:1656, aop.events:341, aop.events:2231 ;
	aopo:has_key_event_relationship	aop.relationships:3651, aop.relationships:3652, aop.relationships:3653, aop.relationships:3654, aop.relationships:3655, aop.relationships:448, aop.relationships:3656, aop.relationships:3657, aop.relationships:3659, aop.relationships:3660, aop.relationships:3661, aop.relationships:3662, aop.relationships:3663, aop.relationships:3664, aop.relationships:3665, aop.relationships:3666, aop.relationships:3192, aop.relationships:3667, aop.relationships:3658 ;
