aop.events:1438
	a	aopo:KeyEvent ;
	dc:identifier	aop.events:1438 ;
	rdfs:label	"KE 1438" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.events/1438> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.events/1438> ;
	dc:title	"Transcription of genes encoding acute phase proteins, Increased" ;
	dcterms:alternative	"Increased transcription of genes encoding acute phase proteins" ;
	dc:source	"AOPWiki" ;
	dc:description	"""Acute phase proteins (APPs) are proteins that have an increase in plasma concentration of at least 25% during an acute phase response (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999; Mantovani &amp; Garlanda, 2023). In humans, the major APPs are C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A, while in mice the major APPs are serum amyloid A, haptoglobin and serum amyloid P (Cray, Zaias, &amp; Altman, 2009; Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). All animals--from fish to mammals--have APP, but the type of major APP differs by species (Cray, 2012).

It is widely accepted that APPs are mainly produced in the liver, while several other tissues have been shown to express APPs. In humans, APP genes have been shown to be expressed in the adrenal gland, adipose tissue, appendix, gall bladder, heart, kidney, liver, lung, placenta, prostate, salivary gland, skin, small intestine, stomach, thymus, thyroid, trachea and uterus (de Dios et al., 2018; NCBI, 2023; Schr&ouml;dl et al., 2016; Urieli-Shoval, Cohen, Eisenberg, &amp; Matzner, 1998; Venteclef, Jakobsson, Steffensen, &amp; Treuter, 2011). In mice, APPs have been shown to be expressed in the adrenal gland, bladder, central nervous system, colon, duodenum, genital fat pad, heart, kidney, large intestine, limbs, liver, lung, mammary gland, ovary, placenta, small intestine, spleen, subcutaneous fat pad, testis and thymus (Kalmovarin et al., 1991; NCBI, 2023; Saber et al., 2013).

Table 1 presents a list of acute phase response genes in humans and mice according the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI): Table 1.

It is important to note that humans and mice express four SAA isoforms (Saa1, Saa2, Saa3 and Saa4), while Saa3 is a pseudogene in humans (Shridas &amp; Tannock, 2019). CRP is expressed in humans and mice, although only moderately expressed in mice (Pepys &amp; Hirschfield, 2003).

In mice, Saa1, Saa2 and Saa3 are all expressed in lung tissue (Saber et al., 2014). Furthermore, the Saa3 isoform is the most differentially expressed APP gene in lung tissue and it is not highly expressed in the liver, while Saa1 gene is the most differentially Saa isoform expressed in liver tissue after exposure to particles (Halappanavar et al., 2011; Poulsen et al., 2017; Saber et al., 2014).
""" ;
	mmo:0000000	"""No suitable test method was identified in TSAR - Tracking System for Alternative methods towards Regulatory acceptance at https://tsar.jrc.ec.europa.eu/type-regulationstandard/oecd (assessed on May 21, 2026). However, a number of other methods are described in the scientific literature: 

Gene expression of acute phase proteins (APPs) can be measured from tissue samples using quantitative Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR). This technique allows the amplification of selected fragments of DNA or cDNA by using primers (i.e. known end-portions of the selected DNA). By repeated cycles of transcription, DNA is amplified. The use of fluorescent probes to quantify the expression the targeted DNA, as the binding of the probe to this DNA emits a fluorescent signal. Sequences of RNA can be quantify using PCR, by first synthetizing DNA from a RNA sample, resulting in cDNA. This technique is called reverse transcriptase PCR (Nelson, Nelson, Lehninger, &amp; Cox, 2017).

Other techniques for evaluating the expression of several APPs at the same time are microarray analysis, total RNA sequencing &nbsp;or single cell sequencing (Halappanavar et al., 2011; Nelson et al., 2017; Voss et al., 2025).

&nbsp;

To evaluate this key event in mice, gene expression of Saa3 can be quantified in lung tissue and Saa1 gene expression in liver tissue after exposure to a stressor (Halappanavar et al., 2011; Poulsen et al., 2017; Saber et al., 2014). 

In humans, it is not common to measure gene expression as a tissue sample is required, however gene expression of crp, saa1, saa2 and saa4 can be measured from samples taken during surgery (Calero et al., 2014).  
""" ;
	nci:C25664	"""Organ""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""
	Taxonomic applicability: Acute phase response&nbsp;is part of the immune response and is observed in vertebrate species (Cray et al., 2009).
	Life stage applicability: This key event is applicable to all life stages (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999; Hansen et al., 2024; O&#39;Donnell et al., 2009).
	Sex applicability: This key event is applicable to male and female sexes (Emerging Risk Factors et al., 2010).

""" ;
	pato:0000047	"Male", "Female" ;
	aopo:LifeStageContext	"All life stages" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	ncbitaxon:10090, "WCS_9606" ;
	aopo:OrganContext	uberon:0002048 ;
	aopo:hasBiologicalEvent	<1438_bioevent_0> ;
	go:0008150	go:0006953 ;
	pato:0000001	"increased" ;
	pato:0001241	"N/A" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:237 .

aop.events:1439
	a	aopo:KeyEvent ;
	dc:identifier	aop.events:1439 ;
	rdfs:label	"KE 1439" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.events/1439> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.events/1439> ;
	dc:title	"Systemic acute phase response" ;
	dcterms:alternative	"Systemic acute phase response" ;
	dc:source	"AOPWiki" ;
	dc:description	"""Acute phase response (APR) is the systemic response to acute and chronic inflammatory states, for example bacterial infection, trauma and infarction. Acute phase proteins (APP) have been shown to be valuable biomarkers as increase can occur with inflammation, infection, neoplasia, stress, and trauma (Cray, 2012). Proteins are considered APP when their plasma concentration changes at least 25% (up or down) in response to these stressors &nbsp;(Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). All animals--from fish to mammals--have APP, but the type of major APP differs by species (Cray, 2012). 

APPs that increase in concentration during APR are called positive APP, while negative APP are decreased during APR (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). In humans, the two major positive APPs are C-reactive protein (CRP) and serum amyloid A (SAA), whose concentration can increase more than 1000-fold during an acute phase response (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). Additional human acute phase proteins include &nbsp;Alpha-1 antitrypsin, albumin, alpha-2 macroglobulin, ceruloplasmin, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, serum amyloid P, and transferrin (Cray, 2012). In mice, the major positive APPs (i.e. the APPs whose plasma concentration changes the most during APR) are SAA, haptoglobin and serum amyloid P (Cray, Zaias, &amp; Altman, 2009).

APPs are detected in the blood. The organ of biosynthesis of the APPs may vary, and increased mRNA levels of acute phase genes in organ tissue may provide evidence of APP expression in a given organ. Traditionally, the liver has been regarded as a major site of APP biosynthesis (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). However, other organs such as the lung may express APP following exposure to stressors (Saber et al., 2013). In addition, acute phase response is also characterized by fever and leucocytosis (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999).
""" ;
	mmo:0000000	"""No suitable test method was identified in&nbsp; TSAR - Tracking System for Alternative methods towards Regulatory acceptance at https://tsar.jrc.ec.europa.eu/type-regulationstandard/oecd (assessed on May 21, 2026).

&nbsp;

Other methods include:

&nbsp;

Systemic acute phase response is assessed by measuring acute phase protein (APP) concentrations in blood, plasma or serum. 

&nbsp;

In mice, serum amyloid A (SAA) isoforms are measured using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) assays or Western blot &nbsp;(Christophersen et al., 2021; Gutierrez et al., 2023; Hadrup et al., 2019; Halappanavar et al., 2011; Poulsen et al., 2017). ELISA assays allows the measurement of proteins in a sample by adsorbing the desired proteins (antigens) to an inert surface. Using antibodies specific to the protein and an enzyme that catalyzes the reaction, a colorimetric signal is produced and measured (Nelson, Nelson, Lehninger, &amp; Cox, 2017). In the case of Western blot, also called immunoblot assay, proteins in a sample are first separated by gel electrophoresis. Following, the proteins are transferred to a membrane and treated with antibodies, obtaining a coloured precipitate along the band of the desired protein (Nelson et al., 2017).

&nbsp;

In humans, most often C-reactive protein (CRP) and SAA are measured. These proteins are measured by immunoassays detecting single or multiple proteins (Adetona et al., 2017; Andersen et al., 2019; Baumann et al., 2018; Meier et al., 2014; Monse et al., 2018; Walker et al., 2022; Wyatt, Devlin, Rappold, Case, &amp; Diaz-Sanchez, 2020). In addition, CRP is measured by turbidimetric (Barregard et al., 2006; Kim, Chen, Boyce, &amp; Christiani, 2005; Sikkeland et al., 2018) and nephelometric assays (Brand et al., 2014). &nbsp;

For both techniques, a blood sample (serum or plasma) is mixed with a suspension of latex beads coated with CRP antibodies. When CRP binds to the beads, a complex is formed that produces the scattering of light. The amount of light scattered is proportional to the amount of complexes formed. While in nephelometry the amount of light scaterred is measure, in turbidimetry the amount of light that passes through the suspension is measured. The measurements are later converted to CRP concentration using a calibration curve (Drieghe, Alsaadi, Tugirimana, &amp; Delanghe, 2014; Hamilton, 2014).

CRP is used in the clinical setting as a marker of systemic inflammation.  
""" ;
	nci:C25664	"""Organ""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""
	Taxonomic applicability: Acute phase response is part of the immune response and is observed in vertebrates (Cray et al., 2009).
	Life stages applicability: This key event is applicable to all life stages (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999; Hansen et al., 2024; Nebbioso et al., 2026; O&#39;Donnell et al., 2009).
	Sex applicability: This key event is applicable to male and female sexes (Emerging Risk Factors et al., 2010).

""" ;
	pato:0000047	"Male", "Female" ;
	aopo:LifeStageContext	"All life stages" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	ncbitaxon:10090, "WCS_9606" ;
	aopo:OrganContext	uberon:0000178 ;
	aopo:hasBiologicalEvent	<1439_bioevent_0> ;
	go:0008150	go:0006953 ;
	pato:0000001	"increased" ;
	pato:0001241	"N/A" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:237 .

