	dc:title	"Increased, histomorphological alteration of testis" ;
	dcterms:alternative	"Increased, histomorphological alteration of testis" ;
	dc:source	"AOPWiki" ;
	dc:description	"""Testicular histomorphological alterations include any and all changes to the morphology of cells and tissues associated with the testis.&nbsp; For example: hemorrhage, edema, fibrosis, necrosis, inflammation, calcification, percent interstitial tissue, lymphatic space volume, nuclei volume, thrombosis, vacuolization, tubular diameter changes, and vasculitis.&nbsp; Histomorphological alterations precede decreases in spermatogenesis and steroidogenesis resulting in reproductive failure (da Silva 2021).&nbsp;
""" ;
	mmo:0000000	"""Can be measured quantitatively or qualitatively in any of the testis constituent tissues and cells including interstitial tissue, seminiferous tubules, lumen, albuginea, tunica propria, Leydig cells, blood vessels, and the germinal epithelium.&nbsp; Techniques mostly use staining or microscopy techniques and include the following examples:


	Annexin-V PI staining
	Spectrophotometric hemoglobin assay
	TGF-&beta;-dependent gene expression

""" ;
	nci:C25664	"""Tissue""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""Taxonomic applicability: AOP521 is focused on murine models but element imbalance and micromineral mimicry are not limited to this taxon.

Life stage applicability: Limited to adult organisms with reproductive tissues and structures.

Sex applicability: Limited to male organisms.

In vitro data is used to support these domains.
""" ;
	pato:0000047	"Male" ;
	aopo:LifeStageContext	"Adult" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	ncbitaxon:39108 ;
	aopo:OrganContext	uberon:0000473 ;
	aopo:hasBiologicalEvent	<2206_bioevent_0> ;
	go:0008150	hp:0000035 ;
	pato:0000001	"increased" ;
	pato:0001241	fma:7210 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:521 .

