aop.relationships:1200
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:1200 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 1200" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1200> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1200> ;
	dcterms:created	"2016-11-29T18:41:37" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2025-11-19T05:35:08" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:1153 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:1154 ;
	nci:C17469	"""Taxonomic:&nbsp;According to the evaluation of the empirical taxonomic domain of applicability (tDOA) of an adverse outcome pathway network for thyroid hormone system disruption (THSD) by Haigis et al., 2023, the level of confidence for a linkage between DIO3 inhibition and altered thyroid hormone (TH) levels was considered high for fish and amphibians (Darras, 2021, Darras and Van Herck, 2012, Fini et al., 2007, Heijlen et al., 2014, Houbrechts et al., 2016, Mayasich et al., 2021, Mol et al., 1998, Noyes et al., 2011, Sanders et al., 1999, Thompson and Cline, 2016) and moderate for mammals (Darras, 2021, Darras and Van Herck, 2012, Hernandez et al., 2006, Ng et al., 2009, Olker et al., 2019). This was supported by structural protein conservation analysis by Lalone et al., 2018 and Haigis et al., 2023. Structural protein conservation of mammalian, fish, amphibian, reptilian and avian DIO3 was found compared to the human (Homo sapiens) protein target using the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency&rsquo;s Sequence Alignment to Predict Across Species Susceptibility (SeqAPASS v6.0; seqapass.epa.gov/seqapass/) tool, while acknowledging the potential existence of interspecies differences in conservation. No empirical evidence linking DIO3 inhibition to THSD was found for reptiles and birds. It should be mentioned that although the level of DIO3 conservation between chicken and the human reference was relatively high, SeqAPASS did not predict DIO3 conservation for birds in general.
""" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	"WCS_8355", "WikiUser_6", ncbitaxon:8292, "WikiUser_17" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:191 .

