aop.relationships:1271
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:1271 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 1271" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1271> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1271> ;
	dcterms:created	"2016-11-29T18:41:37" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2016-11-29T21:13:12" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:1215 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:1218 ;
	dc:description	"""Electrophiles (e.g. isocyanates, aldehydes) react covalently with ankyrin domains while select non-reactive irritants activate through an uncharacterized alternative mechanism.  Both cause localized calcium transients and deplorarization of neuronal membrane which may lead to action potentials.
""" ;
	nci:C80263	"""The basic function of TRPA1 is known and is concordant with existing biological knowledge.
""" ;
	edam:data_2042	"""Extensive evidence from in vitro patch clamp and in vivo toxicity studies show that TRPA1 is molecularly sensitive to electrophiles, noxious cold, and other irritants (Jordt et al, 2004, Hinman et al, 2006, Macpherson et al, 2007, Richards et al, 2010).
""" ;
	nci:C71478	"""Some modes of TRPA1 activation are unclear.
""" ;
	edam:operation_3799	"""Threshold concentrations (EC50 values) for channel activation and action potential generation are known in the case of many TRPA1 agonists.  TRPV1 sensitivity may also be regulated by various other signaling events.
""" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:196 .

aop.relationships:1274
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:1274 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 1274" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1274> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/1274> ;
