aop.relationships:3219
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:3219 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 3219" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3219> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3219> ;
	dcterms:created	"2024-05-14T13:55:26" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2026-08-13T10:56:45" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:18 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:459 ;
	dc:description	"""The aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR) is a ligand-activated transcription factor that mediates cellular responses to environmental toxins, dietary components, and endogenous metabolites. Upon ligand binding, AhR translocates to the nucleus, dimerizes with the aryl hydrocarbon receptor nuclear translocator (ARNT), and binds to xenobiotic response elements (XREs) in the promoter regions of target genes. While its primary function involves detoxification through the regulation of cytochrome P450 enzymes (e.g., CYP1A1, CYP1B1), chronic or excessive AhR activation is implicated in metabolic disorders, including liver steatosis (PMID:&nbsp;34830313, PMID:&nbsp;37284280).&nbsp; 

AhR activation modulates lipid homeostasis through the upregulation of key lipogenic pathways. It regulates the expression of sterol regulatory element-binding protein 1c (SREBP-1c), a master regulator of fatty acid and triglyceride synthesis (PMID:&nbsp;29694888). Increased SREBP-1c expression enhances the transcription of lipogenic enzymes, such as acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) and fatty acid synthase (FASN), leading to de novo lipogenesis (PMID:&nbsp;21029304). Concurrently, AhR increases the expression of CD36, a key transporter of fatty acids, which contributes to the import of these molecules in the cytoplasm and subsequently to de novo lipogenesis (PMID:&nbsp;20303349).&nbsp; 

AhR activation also drives liver steatosis by inducing inflammatory responses. It promotes the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines, such as interleukin-6 (IL-6) and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-&alpha;) (PMID:&nbsp;27713108). This inflammatory milieu exacerbates hepatic lipid accumulation by impairing insulin signaling, which further upregulates SREBP-1c activity. Additionally, AhR activation increases the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS), leading to oxidative stress, lipid peroxidation, and hepatocyte injury, all of which contribute to the pathogenesis of steatosis (PMID:&nbsp;34830313).&nbsp; Furthermore, AhR-mediated repression of fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF21), a hormone critical for lipid metabolism and insulin sensitivity, has been implicated in exacerbating steatosis (PMID:&nbsp;27226639).&nbsp; 
""" ;
	nci:C80263	"""Activation of AhR can directly alter hepatic lipid homeostasis through several mechanistically interconnected processes. In mice, constitutive hepatic AhR activation induces spontaneous triglyceride accumulation and steatosis, associated with increased expression of the fatty-acid transporter CD36 and other fatty-acid transport proteins, decreased fatty-acid oxidation, reduced hepatic triglyceride export and increased oxidative stress. CD36 has been identified as a direct transcriptional target of AhR, and deletion of CD36 markedly attenuates the steatotic effect of AhR agonism, providing experimental support for a causal AhR&ndash;lipid accumulation relationship (PMID: 20303349)

Additional loss-of-function evidence supports this relationship. AhR-deficient mice are protected against high-fat-diet-induced hepatic steatosis, and display altered expression of genes involved in fatty-acid uptake and lipid metabolism. In another experimental model, activation of the AhR&ndash;CD36 axis increased hepatocyte lipid uptake and hepatic steatosis, whereas AhR knockdown or pharmacological antagonism reduced lipid accumulation (PMID: 25907315)

Taken together, these findings provide a biologically plausible mechanism whereby sustained hepatic AhR activation can shift the balance between fatty-acid uptake, synthesis, oxidation and export toward hepatic lipid accumulation.
""" ;
	edam:data_2042	"""There is temporal concordance as AhR activation leads to adaptative responses in the liver including metabolic switch. In tumor cells, AhR activation is associated with a Warburg effect and increased glycolytic activity; subsequently, mitochondrial metabolism is decreased which is consistent with liver steatosis that depends on lipid catabolism mainly observed in the mitochondrial matrix with beta-oxidation (PMID:&nbsp;26582802).
""" ;
	nci:C71478	"""The relationship is context-dependent. AhR is activated by structurally and functionally diverse endogenous and exogenous ligands, and not all forms of AhR activation promote steatosis. For example, the endogenous AhR agonist cinnabarinic acid has been reported to reduce hepatic lipid uptake and steatosis in experimental models through an AhR-dependent mechanism. Thus, the direction and magnitude of the metabolic response may depend on ligand identity, duration and magnitude of AhR activation, tissue and cellular context, and underlying metabolic status (PMID: 35933113).
""" ;
	nci:C103159	"""Evidence supporting this KER was identified through a targeted literature search of PubMed, complemented by reference tracking from relevant primary studies and existing AOP-Wiki entries. Searches were last updated in August 2026 and used combinations of the terms &ldquo;aryl hydrocarbon receptor&rdquo;, &ldquo;AhR&rdquo; or &ldquo;AHR&rdquo; with &ldquo;hepatic steatosis&rdquo;, &ldquo;fatty liver&rdquo;, &ldquo;NAFLD&rdquo;, &ldquo;MASLD&rdquo;, &ldquo;hepatic lipid accumulation&rdquo;, &ldquo;triglyceride&rdquo;, &ldquo;CD36&rdquo;, &ldquo;fatty acid uptake&rdquo; and &ldquo;fatty acid oxidation&rdquo;. Studies were prioritized when they simultaneously assessed AhR activation or inhibition and hepatic lipid accumulation and when they included genetic or pharmacological manipulation of AhR or downstream mediators. Evidence was considered across mammalian in vivo models and human-relevant hepatic in vitro systems. Particular attention was paid to studies providing mechanistic evidence, temporal or dose-response information, and loss-of-function or rescue experiments. Reviews were used to identify additional primary literature but were not considered primary empirical evidence.
""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""
	Human&nbsp;: PMID:&nbsp;31401392&nbsp;
	Rat: PMID:&nbsp;34848246
	Mouse&nbsp;: PMID:&nbsp;34830313, PMID:&nbsp;27713108

""" ;
	edam:operation_3799	"""There are no quantitative data.
""" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	"WCS_9606", ncbitaxon:10116, ncbitaxon:10090 ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:494 .

aop.relationships:3218
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:3218 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 3218" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3218> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3218> ;
	dcterms:created	"2024-05-14T13:53:04" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2025-01-30T12:34:59" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:459 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:55 ;
	dc:description	"""Liver steatosis involves the abnormal accumulation of lipids within hepatocytes, which, when excessive, induces cellular stress through mechanisms such as oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and endoplasmic reticulum stress. These disruptions compromise cell integrity and function, leading to cell death through apoptosis or necrosis. Indeed, empirical evidence supports this relationship (see below also) across various models, including in vitro studies where lipid accumulation triggers reactive oxygen species (ROS) production and cell death, as well as in vivo models of diet-induced steatosis that demonstrate progressive liver damage. Clinically, in patients with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), a transition from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) involves hepatocyte injury and death, evidenced by histopathological changes and increased biomarkers of liver damage (PMID: 34848246).

The quantitative link between lipid accumulation and cell injury/death shows a dose-dependent relationship, although modulating factors such as the type of lipids, genetic predispositions, and environmental influences can affect the severity and onset of cell damage. Understanding this KER is essential for identifying interventions aimed at mitigating the progression of liver steatosis to irreversible liver injury.
""" ;
	nci:C80263	"""
	Liver steatosis, characterized by excessive accumulation of lipids in hepatocytes, predisposes cells to injury and death due to lipotoxicity. Excessive lipid accumulation leads to oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and endoplasmic reticulum stress. These cellular disturbances compromise membrane integrity, impair energy homeostasis, and activate apoptotic and necrotic pathways, resulting in cell injury and death. Chronic steatosis amplifies inflammatory signaling, creating a self-perpetuating cycle of liver damage (PMID:&nbsp;37400694).

""" ;
	edam:data_2042	"""
	In vitro studies:&nbsp;Hepatocytes exposed to elevated free fatty acid levels (e.g., palmitate) exhibit lipid accumulation followed by markers of oxidative stress (e.g., ROS production) and cell death (apoptosis or necrosis).
	Animal models:&nbsp;Rodents fed a high-fat diet show progression from liver steatosis to hepatocyte injury, evidenced by elevated serum alanine aminotransferase (ALT) levels and histological detection of necrotic foci.
	Clinical observations:&nbsp;In patients with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), progression from simple steatosis to non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) involves significant hepatocyte ballooning (a marker of injury) and cell death

""" ;
	nci:C71478	"""
	Heterogeneity of lipid species:&nbsp;Not all lipid accumulations are equally toxic. For example, triglycerides may serve as inert storage, whereas free fatty acids and ceramides are directly cytotoxic. Same remark regarding the type of fatty acids (saturated ones vs omega3)
	Species differences:&nbsp;Rodents and humans may exhibit differences in susceptibility to lipid-induced liver damage.
	Temporal dynamics:&nbsp;The time lag between lipid accumulation and detectable cell injury varies across models, complicating predictions.

""" ;
	nci:C68821	"""

	
		
			Modulating Factor (MF)
			MF Specification
			Effect(s) on the KER
			Reference(s)
		
	
	
		
			
			Genetic factors
			
			&nbsp;Variants in genes such as PNPLA3 influence susceptibility to lipid-induced hepatocyte injury.
			&nbsp;
			&nbsp;
		
		
			
			Dietary factors
			
			Antioxidants or omega-3 fatty acids may mitigate the progression from steatosis to cell death.
			&nbsp;
			&nbsp;
		
		
			Comorbidities
			&nbsp;Insulin resistance and chronic inflammation exacerbate steatosis-related cell injury.
			&nbsp;
			&nbsp;
		
	



&nbsp;
""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""
	Human&nbsp;: PMID:&nbsp;32044315
	Rat: PMID:&nbsp;26674288
	Mouse&nbsp;: PMID:&nbsp;36690638


Understanding this KER is critical for the development of interventions targeting early lipid accumulation to prevent downstream liver damage. It also aids in the establishment of biomarkers predictive of NAFLD/NASH progression.
""" ;
	edam:operation_3799	"""The relationship between liver steatosis and cell injury/death exhibits a dose-dependent response. Higher levels of lipid accumulation correlate with increased markers of hepatocyte damage, such as ALT, AST, and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) release. Thresholds for lipid overload beyond which cellular injury becomes inevitable have been proposed in experimental studies but remain context-dependent, influenced by factors like lipid species and duration of exposure.
""" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:494 .

