aop.relationships:3291
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:3291 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 3291" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3291> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3291> ;
	dcterms:created	"2024-07-08T14:16:34" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2024-12-03T10:18:46" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:2238 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:2239 ;
	dc:description	"""Increased blood viscosity results in increased cardiac stress because of increased resistance to flow, increasing cardiac workload to circulate blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;
""" ;
	nci:C80263	"""The relationship between blood viscosity and cardiac stress has been studied in the context of osmoregulation and stressors that affect ion concentrations, fluid movement across membranes, and related changes to fluid volume and resulting blood viscosity. &nbsp;Due to fluid dynamics there is a well-established relationship between viscosity and resistance to flow, with corresponding increases in cardiac stress when attempting to move more viscous blood.
""" ;
	edam:data_2042	"""
	
		
			
			Species
			
			
			Duration
			
			
			Dose
			
			
			Increased blood viscosity?
			
			
			Increased cardiac stress?
			
			
			Summary
			
			
			Citation
			
		
		
			
			Rainbow trout (Salmo gairdneri)
			
			
			3 days
			
			
			low pH 4.31
			
			
			yes
			
			
			yes
			
			
			Adult trout showed statistically significant increased blood viscosity and hematocrit and resulting cardiac stress with statistically significant increased heart rate, dorsal aortic blood pressure.
			
			
			Milligan and Wood (1982)
			
		
		
			
			Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss)
			
			
			24 hours
			
			
			4.9 umol/L copper
			
			
			yes
			
			
			yes
			
			
			Adult trout showed increased blood viscosity with statistically significant increased hematocrit and resulting cardiac stress with statistically significant increased heart rate, dorsal aortic blood pressure.
			
			
			Wilson and Taylor (1993)
			
		
		
			
			Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss)
			
			
			60 hours
			
			
			20,40,80 ug/L aluminum chloride, low pH 5.1.
			
			
			yes 
			
			
			yes
			
			
			Rainbow trout showed dose dependent increased blood viscosity with statistically significant increased hematocrit at low pH and 80 ug/L and up to 60% increased blood viscosity and resulting cardiac stress with statistically significant increased heart rate and decreased stroke volume.
			
			
			Dussault et al. (2001)
			
		
	

""" ;
	nci:C103159	"""This Key Event Relationship was developed as part of an Environmental Protection Agency effort to represent putative AOPs from peer-reviewed literature which were heretofore unrepresented in the AOP-Wiki. &nbsp;Brix et al. (2022) focused on identifying Adverse Outcome Pathways associated with chronic copper exposure in aquatic vertebrates through review of existing literature, and provided initial network analysis.&nbsp;

Cited empirical studies are focused on blood viscosity and cardiac stress in freshwater fish, in support of development of AOP 539 for Brix et al. (2022) content.&nbsp; &nbsp;


Authors of KER 3291 did a further evaluation of published peer-reviewed literature to provide additional evidence in support of the key event relationship.
&nbsp;
""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""Life Stage: Applies to all life stages; not specific to any life stage.


Sex: Applies to both males and females; not sex-specific.


Taxonomic: Freshwater true chordates, with evidence primarily from fish.
""" ;
	pato:0000047	"Unspecific" ;
	aopo:LifeStageContext	"All life stages" ;
	ncbitaxon:131567	"WikiUser_6" ;
	dcterms:isPartOf	aop:539 .

aop.relationships:3292
	a	aopo:KeyEventRelationship ;
	dc:identifier	aop.relationships:3292 ;
	rdfs:label	"KER 3292" ;
	foaf:page	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3292> ;
	rdfs:seeAlso	<https://identifiers.org/aop.relationships/3292> ;
	dcterms:created	"2024-07-08T14:16:58" ;
	dcterms:modified	"2024-12-03T10:19:03" ;
	aopo:has_upstream_key_event	aop.events:2239 ;
	aopo:has_downstream_key_event	aop.events:1535 ;
	dc:description	"""Cardiac stress results in increased blood pressure, heart rate, and stroke volume, with increased probability of heart failure.&nbsp; While short-term increases in heart workload due to bursts in activity are normal, here we are focused on chronic cardiac stress leading to cardiac failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;Increased risk of heart failure is generally inferred rather than increasing cardiac stress to the point of heart failure and mortality.


&nbsp;
""" ;
	nci:C80263	"""Cardiac stress has been studied by increases in physical activity, as well as chemical stressors.&nbsp; Chronic cardiac stress causes greater risk of heart failure due to overwork, damage, and exertion demands exceeding heart capabilities.&nbsp;
""" ;
	edam:data_2042	"""
	
		
			
			Species
			
			
			Duration
			
			
			Dose
			
			
			Increased cardiac stress?
			
			
			Increased heart failure?
			
			
			Summary
			
			
			Citation
			
		
		
			
			Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss)
			
			
			24 hours
			
			
			4.9 umol/L copper
			
			
			yes
			
			
			yes
			
			
			Adult trout showed increased cardiac stress with statistically significant increased heart rate, dorsal aortic blood pressure and mortality for all exposed fish.
			
			
			Wilson and Taylor (1993)
			
		
	

""" ;
	nci:C103159	"""This Key Event Relationship was developed as part of an Environmental Protection Agency effort to represent putative AOPs from peer-reviewed literature which were heretofore unrepresented in the AOP-Wiki. &nbsp;Brix et al. (2022) focused on identifying Adverse Outcome Pathways associated with chronic copper exposure in aquatic vertebrates through review of existing literature, and provided initial network analysis.

Cited empirical studies are focused on blood viscosity and cardiac stress in freshwater fish, in support of development of AOP 539 for Brix et al. (2022) content.&nbsp; &nbsp;


Authors of KER 3292 did a further evaluation of published peer-reviewed literature to provide additional evidence in support of the key event relationship.
&nbsp;
""" ;
	nci:C17469	"""Life Stage: Applies to all life stages; not specific to any life stage.


Sex: Applies to both males and females; not sex-specific.


Taxonomic: Freshwater true chordates, with evidence primarily from fish.&nbsp;
""" ;
